  "content": {
    "id": "p54-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "Prove that $(x^2-4)$ is a factor of $P(x)=x^4+2x^3-3x^2-8x-4$ without performing a division.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2)$ and $P(2)=P(-2)=0$, so $x^2-4$ is a factor.",
      "worked": "$x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(2)&=2^4+2(2)^3-3(2)^2-8(2)-4\\\\P(2)&=16+16-12-16-4\\\\P(2)&=0\\\\P(-2)&=(-2)^4+2(-2)^3-3(-2)^2-8(-2)-4\\\\P(-2)&=16-16-12+16-4\\\\P(-2)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nBy the factor theorem, $x-2$ and $x+2$ are factors. These are distinct linear factors, so their product $x^2-4$ is a factor.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 35
  },
